WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:27.565 --> 00:00:28.398
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:28.398 --> 00:00:30.757
Welcome to our Homeroom livestream,

00:00:30.757 --> 00:00:32.876
Sal Khan here from Khan Academy.

00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:34.755
For those of you who are
wondering what this is,

00:00:34.755 --> 00:00:37.213
this is just something we
started up several months ago,

00:00:37.213 --> 00:00:39.620
especially when we all have
to become socially distanced

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.363
as a way of having interesting topics

00:00:41.363 --> 00:00:44.137
with interesting guests and
also taking your questions.

00:00:44.137 --> 00:00:45.971
And I'm really excited
about our guest today,

00:00:45.971 --> 00:00:47.944
Jacquelline Fuller from Google.org.

00:00:47.944 --> 00:00:49.281
But before that,

00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:51.396
I would like to give a special shout out

00:00:51.396 --> 00:00:53.614
to several organizations that stepped up

00:00:53.614 --> 00:00:55.483
when they realized that Khan Academy

00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:58.014
was already running at
a deficit pre-COVID.

00:00:58.014 --> 00:01:00.763
And our cost only went up
whether it's server costs

00:01:00.763 --> 00:01:02.221
or we're trying to accelerate programs

00:01:02.221 --> 00:01:04.810
or adding new content in
response to school closure.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.150
So special, thanks to Google.org,

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.513
Bank of America, AT&amp;T,
Fastly and Novartis.

00:01:10.513 --> 00:01:13.219
I will say to all of
you listening out there

00:01:13.219 --> 00:01:16.209
as much as help this has
been from the support of

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:18.021
these great organizations.

00:01:18.021 --> 00:01:20.689
We are still running at
a deficit and as we know

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:23.108
the school closure
issue, isn't going away.

00:01:23.108 --> 00:01:24.492
So we're trying to do what we can,

00:01:24.492 --> 00:01:26.007
but if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:26.007 --> 00:01:28.969
please think about going
to Khanacademy.org/donate.

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:31.786
We only exist because of
philanthropic donations

00:01:31.786 --> 00:01:33.660
from folks like yourself.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.829
So any level of donation makes a big deal.

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:40.825
So please think about doing that.

00:01:40.825 --> 00:01:44.671
So with that, I'm excited
about introducing our guest

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:48.234
Jacquelline Fuller
president of Google.org,

00:01:48.234 --> 00:01:50.830
Jacquelline great to have you here.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:54.650
- Great to be here to chat with you Sal.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.956
- And maybe a good place to start.

00:01:56.956 --> 00:01:59.088
Obviously everyone knows Google,

00:01:59.088 --> 00:02:01.172
some folks know Google.org.

00:02:01.172 --> 00:02:02.351
What is Google.org?

00:02:02.351 --> 00:02:03.430
Why does it exist?

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.950
What does the.org mean?

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.791
- So Google.org is Google's philanthropy

00:02:08.791 --> 00:02:11.251
and essentially we're
a part of the company.

00:02:11.251 --> 00:02:14.241
We're a team embedded fully within Google.

00:02:14.241 --> 00:02:15.988
And the idea is to think about

00:02:15.988 --> 00:02:19.239
how can we use the unique
strengths of Google

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:21.669
to make the world better?

00:02:21.669 --> 00:02:22.823
And specifically,

00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:27.447
how can we address issues
like education, inclusion?

00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:30.723
More recently taking on
issues like the COVID crisis,

00:02:30.723 --> 00:02:32.424
by doing so in a way that brings together

00:02:32.424 --> 00:02:35.139
the best of Google, all of our assets.

00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:39.110
So we're funded through a 1%
commitment of our companies

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.436
and our profits each year.

00:02:40.436 --> 00:02:43.657
So it's funding North of $200 million,

00:02:43.657 --> 00:02:47.492
but we also think about
Google's strengths in technology

00:02:47.492 --> 00:02:51.997
and our Googlers are
amazingly passionate employees

00:02:51.997 --> 00:02:53.141
who also wanna step in.

00:02:53.141 --> 00:02:55.762
And so we often combine teams of Googlers

00:02:55.762 --> 00:02:57.978
who go and work alongside nonprofits,

00:02:57.978 --> 00:02:59.170
along with funding,

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:03.281
along with our reach and
our voice and advocacy.

00:03:03.281 --> 00:03:04.681
- And just to be clear,

00:03:04.681 --> 00:03:05.973
I mean, this is a little
bit of a disclosure,

00:03:05.973 --> 00:03:08.948
but also I wanna give
you and Google credit

00:03:08.948 --> 00:03:10.638
for all of those who enjoy Khan Academy.

00:03:10.638 --> 00:03:15.555
A lot of people don't realize
Khan Academy back in 2010 ...

00:03:16.486 --> 00:03:17.479
Actually was 2009.

00:03:17.479 --> 00:03:18.312
When we started ...

00:03:18.312 --> 00:03:20.414
This conversation started
when it was just me

00:03:20.414 --> 00:03:24.453
actually in the same walk-in-closet
that I'm in right now.

00:03:24.453 --> 00:03:26.659
The folks at Google
y'all were down the road,

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:28.678
about three miles down
the road talking about

00:03:28.678 --> 00:03:30.418
ways to educate the world.

00:03:30.418 --> 00:03:32.060
There was this ten to
the hundredth project

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.679
and Khan Academy was one of those projects

00:03:35.679 --> 00:03:38.067
and y'all gave a significant
donation at the time ...

00:03:38.067 --> 00:03:40.206
I mean, it's significant today too.

00:03:40.206 --> 00:03:41.623
It was a $2 million donation

00:03:41.623 --> 00:03:44.943
that essentially allowed
Khan Academy to exist.

00:03:44.943 --> 00:03:47.331
And so I don't wanna
embarrass you too much

00:03:47.331 --> 00:03:51.596
with too much praise, but we
would not exist from that.

00:03:51.596 --> 00:03:55.340
And then since then
Google.org has been sponsor

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.967
or a supporter of multiple
projects at Khan Academy.

00:03:58.967 --> 00:04:00.736
But Jacquelline, maybe just ...

00:04:00.736 --> 00:04:04.235
I encourage everyone watching
to ask any questions you have

00:04:04.235 --> 00:04:07.398
for Jacquelline or myself
on YouTube or on Facebook,

00:04:07.398 --> 00:04:08.646
and I'll surface them,

00:04:08.646 --> 00:04:10.603
but Jacquelline maybe to
make it more tangible,

00:04:10.603 --> 00:04:11.987
give examples of the types of stuff

00:04:11.987 --> 00:04:13.860
I gave one my favorite example

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:14.693
(laughing)

00:04:14.693 --> 00:04:16.027
y'all have supported,

00:04:16.027 --> 00:04:18.398
but what other types of things do y'all do

00:04:18.398 --> 00:04:21.676
either in kind of the
traditional philanthropic support

00:04:21.676 --> 00:04:25.941
or in that Google has special
skills type of support?

00:04:25.941 --> 00:04:29.096
- Well, one that's very topical
right now that comes to mind

00:04:29.096 --> 00:04:32.793
is we're investing in racial
justice organizations.

00:04:32.793 --> 00:04:36.025
And particularly we've
had a historic commitment

00:04:36.025 --> 00:04:39.192
to helping to fight mass incarceration

00:04:40.208 --> 00:04:43.146
and to look at racial justice reform.

00:04:43.146 --> 00:04:44.869
So about five years ago,

00:04:44.869 --> 00:04:48.986
Justin Steele on my team led
an initiative where we asked,

00:04:48.986 --> 00:04:53.494
"What can Google do uniquely
to help with this issue

00:04:53.494 --> 00:04:56.091
"of deep systemic bias?"

00:04:56.091 --> 00:05:00.393
And so we invested resources
North of $32 million

00:05:00.393 --> 00:05:04.775
in fabulous partners
like Bryan Stevenson org,

00:05:04.775 --> 00:05:06.674
Equal Justice Initiative,

00:05:06.674 --> 00:05:10.209
organizations such as a
Center for Policing Equity

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:11.893
run by dr. Phil Goff.

00:05:11.893 --> 00:05:13.889
And so we invested in organizations

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:17.305
who were bringing
together data and science

00:05:17.305 --> 00:05:19.845
and trying to figure out how can we solve

00:05:19.845 --> 00:05:22.985
some of these issues
that are deeply rooted

00:05:22.985 --> 00:05:24.455
in systemic bias,

00:05:24.455 --> 00:05:27.116
but were having more data and evidence

00:05:27.116 --> 00:05:30.493
and where technology can
really come to the forefront.

00:05:30.493 --> 00:05:34.140
So we had a historic commitment
there and more recently

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:38.732
because of the movement that's
been happening across the U.S

00:05:38.732 --> 00:05:41.522
we really wanted to make sure
that these frontline orgs

00:05:41.522 --> 00:05:43.594
orgs that are led by people of color

00:05:43.594 --> 00:05:46.786
and really looking at
what are the solutions

00:05:46.786 --> 00:05:48.168
that are enhanced.

00:05:48.168 --> 00:05:49.964
So we reinvested in them

00:05:49.964 --> 00:05:54.176
and sent resources and
additional $12 million,

00:05:54.176 --> 00:05:55.898
but also Googlers.

00:05:55.898 --> 00:05:58.179
I mean, we have passionate Googlers

00:05:58.179 --> 00:06:01.838
who have unique skillsets that
they can bring to the table.

00:06:01.838 --> 00:06:05.314
So for example, working
with the Vera Institute,

00:06:05.314 --> 00:06:09.952
a team of Google engineers
and product managers

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:11.005
and UX folks,

00:06:11.005 --> 00:06:15.102
and even marketing folks came
alongside the Vera Institute

00:06:15.102 --> 00:06:18.199
and helped bring together
for the first time ever

00:06:18.199 --> 00:06:22.239
data around jails around the countries.

00:06:22.239 --> 00:06:24.146
We talk a lot about federal prisons,

00:06:24.146 --> 00:06:25.882
but most folks are in jails

00:06:25.882 --> 00:06:28.538
and there's not really one
authoritative data source

00:06:28.538 --> 00:06:31.194
that helps us look at
who's being incarcerated?

00:06:31.194 --> 00:06:33.548
What are the demographic characteristics?

00:06:33.548 --> 00:06:35.997
How many are in, for example,

00:06:35.997 --> 00:06:39.186
just because they can't
pay bonds, jail bonds?

00:06:39.186 --> 00:06:42.548
So that's the kind of work that we do,

00:06:42.548 --> 00:06:45.163
where we empower these
frontline organizations

00:06:45.163 --> 00:06:48.830
to do what they're doing
and help build them

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:52.118
through not only the
funding and the resources,

00:06:52.118 --> 00:06:54.196
but through Googlers.

00:06:54.196 --> 00:06:55.349
- I'm just curious.

00:06:55.349 --> 00:06:57.687
It sounds like y'all have been
doing this for a little while

00:06:57.687 --> 00:06:58.906
and y'all are accelerating some of this

00:06:58.906 --> 00:07:00.963
around social justice, racial justice,

00:07:00.963 --> 00:07:04.801
any kind of surprising insights
out of that about the data.

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:07.028
How many people are there for ...

00:07:07.028 --> 00:07:09.398
I'm just making up stuff,
nonviolent offenses

00:07:09.398 --> 00:07:14.293
or for not being able to pay
bond or things like that.

00:07:14.293 --> 00:07:15.888
- Yeah. I mean, it's fascinating.

00:07:15.888 --> 00:07:18.996
And viewers can go to the
Center for Policing Equity

00:07:18.996 --> 00:07:20.464
or the Vera Institute.

00:07:20.464 --> 00:07:23.315
Vera just published the
data that we helped them

00:07:23.315 --> 00:07:24.948
put together with this product.

00:07:24.948 --> 00:07:29.656
But for example, you see
that the numbers incarcerated

00:07:29.656 --> 00:07:32.105
were pretty steady through about 1980,

00:07:32.105 --> 00:07:36.450
and then it just hits an escal
that looks a lot like COVID

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:38.933
just starts to accelerate and you see

00:07:38.933 --> 00:07:42.683
just how the percentage
that are incarcerated

00:07:44.042 --> 00:07:46.042
and percentages by race.

00:07:47.157 --> 00:07:50.574
So for example, looking at how right now,

00:07:51.573 --> 00:07:55.729
because of COVID, for
example politicians leaders

00:07:55.729 --> 00:07:57.443
are having to think about

00:07:57.443 --> 00:07:59.596
is this a time where we actually release

00:07:59.596 --> 00:08:04.082
some offenders folks who are
in prisons and jails early

00:08:04.082 --> 00:08:06.351
in part because of COVID

00:08:06.351 --> 00:08:10.221
and having the data to do so
in an informed way to say,

00:08:10.221 --> 00:08:12.171
"Hey, these are nonviolent offenders.

00:08:12.171 --> 00:08:17.029
"These are folks who are a
very low risk to society."

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:20.029
So that is an example of two streams

00:08:21.101 --> 00:08:25.904
of very current research and
needs for data coming together.

00:08:25.904 --> 00:08:29.011
COVID and mass incarceration.

00:08:29.011 --> 00:08:29.944
- Yeah. And related to that,

00:08:29.944 --> 00:08:31.109
I have a question here from YouTube,

00:08:31.109 --> 00:08:32.822
Susanna Garcia Dominguez.

00:08:32.822 --> 00:08:34.928
Who's one of our regular
great question askers.

00:08:34.928 --> 00:08:35.761
Says,

00:08:35.761 --> 00:08:37.133
"Hi, Jacquelline, what are some of your

00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:40.986
"innovative projects
dealing with COVID-19?"

00:08:40.986 --> 00:08:44.667
- Well, it's interesting with COVID.

00:08:44.667 --> 00:08:47.179
So my background is I used to
work at the Gates Foundation.

00:08:47.179 --> 00:08:51.994
So I come from the field of
global health originally.

00:08:51.994 --> 00:08:54.748
And it's just amazing how
little we actually know,

00:08:54.748 --> 00:08:58.036
how much data we actually
have on who's being infected,

00:08:58.036 --> 00:08:59.903
how they're being infected.

00:08:59.903 --> 00:09:02.960
And this is particularly
true in the case of COVID

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.512
for underrepresented groups.

00:09:04.512 --> 00:09:07.134
We know that COVID is
impacting, for example,

00:09:07.134 --> 00:09:10.186
the black community and
the Latino community

00:09:10.186 --> 00:09:12.887
at much higher rates than
say the white community.

00:09:12.887 --> 00:09:16.066
We know that they are
black and brown people

00:09:16.066 --> 00:09:18.144
are more likely to be infected.

00:09:18.144 --> 00:09:20.996
They're more likely to be
hospitalized and unfortunately,

00:09:20.996 --> 00:09:22.984
more likely to die as well.

00:09:22.984 --> 00:09:26.378
But the data there to
understand exactly where

00:09:26.378 --> 00:09:29.992
the hotspots and what
interventions are working

00:09:29.992 --> 00:09:30.825
is not there.

00:09:30.825 --> 00:09:34.700
So we just teamed up with the
Morehouse School of Medicine

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:39.517
who has been doing some excellent
work to gather more data,

00:09:39.517 --> 00:09:44.168
to help inform particularly
how COVID is impacting

00:09:44.168 --> 00:09:46.661
members of minority communities.

00:09:46.661 --> 00:09:48.170
So we gave them a million dollar grant,

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:50.540
but we also send, again a team of Googlers

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:52.089
to work with them on the data.

00:09:52.089 --> 00:09:54.336
And one thing that
happened with that project,

00:09:54.336 --> 00:09:56.024
now, that was a fairly recent project.

00:09:56.024 --> 00:09:58.279
And about the last month or so,

00:09:58.279 --> 00:10:02.097
and particularly at the from
the Google expertise side,

00:10:02.097 --> 00:10:03.995
helping with the use of AI,

00:10:03.995 --> 00:10:06.181
Artificial Intelligence
and Machine learning,

00:10:06.181 --> 00:10:07.902
to make sure that we're taking that data

00:10:07.902 --> 00:10:09.104
and helping the teams there,

00:10:09.104 --> 00:10:12.273
turn that into actionable information.

00:10:12.273 --> 00:10:14.120
But the Department of
Health and Human Services

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:18.189
came alongside us in that
project very quickly afterwards

00:10:18.189 --> 00:10:21.429
and invested an additional 40 million.

00:10:21.429 --> 00:10:24.660
And I think that that's a good
example of what philanthropy

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:27.249
can do because at the end of the day,

00:10:27.249 --> 00:10:30.833
the real engines of change,
the real engines of scale

00:10:30.833 --> 00:10:33.333
are the market and government.

00:10:34.418 --> 00:10:37.118
But I think philanthropy can play a role

00:10:37.118 --> 00:10:38.795
where we're more catalytic

00:10:38.795 --> 00:10:41.197
where we can help really at the beginning.

00:10:41.197 --> 00:10:44.239
Kind of like we did with UCL
to help give that seed funding

00:10:44.239 --> 00:10:47.252
at the beginning to say,
this is a brilliant idea.

00:10:47.252 --> 00:10:48.584
We love Khan Academy.

00:10:48.584 --> 00:10:49.967
We believe in it.

00:10:49.967 --> 00:10:52.202
Let's help invest in it in the beginning.

00:10:52.202 --> 00:10:54.770
Let's help send technology teams as well.

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:58.349
So we're trying to do
that with COVID as well.

00:10:58.349 --> 00:10:59.298
- Yeah, no, it's super,

00:10:59.298 --> 00:11:00.638
and there's a bunch of
questions coming in.

00:11:00.638 --> 00:11:02.199
But actually it would be interesting ...

00:11:02.199 --> 00:11:04.780
I think one of the fun things
about this live stream is

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:06.426
we have a lot of young people watching.

00:11:06.426 --> 00:11:08.517
They're trying to figure
out their own lives.

00:11:08.517 --> 00:11:11.570
I remember being young,
I guess I'm not anymore.

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:14.353
And looking at people that
seem successful and say,

00:11:14.353 --> 00:11:15.470
"How did they get there?"

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.666
So maybe it'd be fun to
take a little step back,

00:11:17.666 --> 00:11:20.156
but then I do wanna ask
where you hope Google

00:11:20.156 --> 00:11:22.949
and Google.org goes, but
just your own personal,

00:11:22.949 --> 00:11:25.652
how did you get into this career?

00:11:25.652 --> 00:11:29.187
Is this a career like running a foundation

00:11:29.187 --> 00:11:32.678
or a corporate foundation or philanthropy?

00:11:32.678 --> 00:11:34.080
I mean, it is a career, but like

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:36.661
there's no major in college around it.

00:11:36.661 --> 00:11:37.941
So tell us a little bit about

00:11:37.941 --> 00:11:39.910
what you thought you
were gonna do growing up

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:43.410
and how that evolved to where you are now.

00:11:44.541 --> 00:11:48.982
- Yes, I did not grow up as a
little girl thinking to myself

00:11:48.982 --> 00:11:52.472
someday I wanna run
corporate philanthropy.

00:11:52.472 --> 00:11:57.472
So this was a bit of a zigzag
trail to get to where I am.

00:11:57.634 --> 00:12:01.680
And so I actually
started when I was young,

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:04.933
having a very strong
commitment to social justice

00:12:04.933 --> 00:12:07.466
and not really understanding or knowing

00:12:07.466 --> 00:12:09.874
how I could best make an impact.

00:12:09.874 --> 00:12:12.230
But just having that as
kind of my Northern Star,

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:15.925
like I knew I really wanted
to invest myself and my time

00:12:15.925 --> 00:12:19.314
and my effort, my skills into an area

00:12:19.314 --> 00:12:21.497
where I was moving the
needle on social justice.

00:12:21.497 --> 00:12:23.340
So I actually started
right out of college.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:26.013
I went to work in the
government because at the time,

00:12:26.013 --> 00:12:28.037
if you wanted to change things at scale,

00:12:28.037 --> 00:12:29.018
you joined the government.

00:12:29.018 --> 00:12:30.046
So I worked actually at

00:12:30.046 --> 00:12:33.255
the Department of Health
and Human Services

00:12:33.255 --> 00:12:35.258
in Washington, DC and I was,

00:12:35.258 --> 00:12:38.607
I think the most junior
person you could possibly hire

00:12:38.607 --> 00:12:42.366
at the time, but I worked on
issues of health disparities

00:12:42.366 --> 00:12:45.555
actually, with minority
communities and under

00:12:45.555 --> 00:12:49.151
Dr. Louis Sullivan, who was
the Head of HHS at the time

00:12:49.151 --> 00:12:51.408
and was actually
coincidentally the founder of

00:12:51.408 --> 00:12:53.849
the Morehouse School of Medicine.

00:12:53.849 --> 00:12:55.330
So, coming full circle.

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:57.394
So I worked in government
for a couple of years,

00:12:57.394 --> 00:13:00.311
and then I thought I really
would like to invest in myself,

00:13:00.311 --> 00:13:02.986
deepen my understanding, my knowledge,

00:13:02.986 --> 00:13:04.734
my technical skills as well.

00:13:04.734 --> 00:13:06.947
So I went and got a
master's in public policy

00:13:06.947 --> 00:13:09.327
from the Kennedy School of Government.

00:13:09.327 --> 00:13:12.994
And while I was there
actually got pregnant.

00:13:14.756 --> 00:13:16.859
And my husband and I had a nice surprise

00:13:16.859 --> 00:13:18.408
when I was in graduate school.

00:13:18.408 --> 00:13:20.816
So we had our first daughter, Hosanna.

00:13:20.816 --> 00:13:24.036
So I took some time where I
really focused on being a mom

00:13:24.036 --> 00:13:25.371
to two young kids.

00:13:25.371 --> 00:13:28.940
And then when I went back
to work after a few years,

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:31.453
there's this new startup,

00:13:31.453 --> 00:13:33.945
we were living in Seattle at the time,

00:13:33.945 --> 00:13:36.278
called the Gates Foundation.

00:13:38.769 --> 00:13:41.352
I read an article where Bill Gates said,

00:13:41.352 --> 00:13:43.816
"I'm gonna give the
majority of my money away

00:13:43.816 --> 00:13:47.585
"and I'm gonna give it to
help the poorest of the poor."

00:13:47.585 --> 00:13:49.031
And I just thought,

00:13:49.031 --> 00:13:51.296
"Hire me. I will help you."

00:13:51.296 --> 00:13:52.787
(laughing)

00:13:52.787 --> 00:13:56.879
So I threw myself at the
gates of the Gates Foundation

00:13:56.879 --> 00:13:58.529
and eventually got a job there.

00:13:58.529 --> 00:14:00.106
I was there for about eight years,

00:14:00.106 --> 00:14:03.196
and then I heard about
another crazy startup

00:14:03.196 --> 00:14:04.276
called Google.org.

00:14:04.276 --> 00:14:05.649
And I thought,

00:14:05.649 --> 00:14:08.279
"Okay, I've worked in government

00:14:08.279 --> 00:14:09.909
"and private philanthropy.

00:14:09.909 --> 00:14:11.851
"I've never actually worked in a company.

00:14:11.851 --> 00:14:14.596
"I'd like to see what companies can do.

00:14:14.596 --> 00:14:17.271
"And this sounds like a
very interesting startup."

00:14:17.271 --> 00:14:21.902
So I came down to join
Google.org about 12 years ago.

00:14:21.902 --> 00:14:22.735
- Yeah.

00:14:22.735 --> 00:14:26.926
And your path is very connected
to us because back in 2010,

00:14:26.926 --> 00:14:30.152
the two organizations that
frankly allowed Khan Academy

00:14:30.152 --> 00:14:31.337
to become a real organization were

00:14:31.337 --> 00:14:34.712
Google and the Gates Foundation
with that first round.

00:14:34.712 --> 00:14:37.762
And then have obviously since
then been great supporters,

00:14:37.762 --> 00:14:39.268
it's all connected.

00:14:39.268 --> 00:14:41.556
- It's all connected.

00:14:41.556 --> 00:14:44.870
- What do you think has
kind of contributed to

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:46.216
your success here?

00:14:46.216 --> 00:14:50.294
What do you think has made
you good at what you do?

00:14:50.294 --> 00:14:53.656
- It's not really boring when I say this,

00:14:53.656 --> 00:14:55.980
but number one is hard work.

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:59.313
I think I had to outwork a lot of people

00:15:02.662 --> 00:15:07.530
to get to where I am and
be smarter and be better

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:10.530
and be more resilient and have grit.

00:15:11.657 --> 00:15:13.979
And I think that's kind of been

00:15:13.979 --> 00:15:16.250
a new boutique area of study.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.278
Angela, I think Duckworth and others

00:15:18.278 --> 00:15:20.060
have really looked into-

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:21.351
- Former guest.

00:15:21.351 --> 00:15:23.273
- Oh, she's fabulous.

00:15:23.273 --> 00:15:26.070
And have really done the
research studies on grit

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:28.808
and how important of a
characteristic that is.

00:15:28.808 --> 00:15:30.876
But I mentioned, for example,

00:15:30.876 --> 00:15:34.353
when I was at the Kennedy School
I had my daughter Hosanna,

00:15:34.353 --> 00:15:37.841
in the middle of my second
year and Kennedy School said,

00:15:37.841 --> 00:15:39.262
"We recommend you leave."

00:15:39.262 --> 00:15:40.245
And I said,

00:15:40.245 --> 00:15:42.008
"No, I think I'm gonna graduate."

00:15:42.008 --> 00:15:44.659
And they gave me no parental leave.

00:15:44.659 --> 00:15:46.312
There was no such thing at the time.

00:15:46.312 --> 00:15:47.652
It didn't actually exist.

00:15:47.652 --> 00:15:49.918
And so I had her on a
Thursday and I went back to

00:15:49.918 --> 00:15:51.660
school on Monday and I got-

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:52.604
- Wow.

00:15:52.604 --> 00:15:54.075
- I had gotten no break.

00:15:54.075 --> 00:15:56.348
I had to finish every single thesis

00:15:56.348 --> 00:15:58.598
and experience and whatnot.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:02.454
So there have been times
in my career where I think

00:16:02.454 --> 00:16:05.667
really digging deep, having
that grit and perseverance

00:16:05.667 --> 00:16:08.137
and hard work is super important.

00:16:08.137 --> 00:16:09.899
I would say the other thing Sal,

00:16:09.899 --> 00:16:13.860
is I just have always
run towards my energy.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:17.943
I've always run towards
what really lights me up.

00:16:19.419 --> 00:16:21.912
We hear a lot about follow your
passion, but at some level,

00:16:21.912 --> 00:16:23.765
I think it's actually true.

00:16:23.765 --> 00:16:26.954
Find that source of energy
that really is gonna

00:16:26.954 --> 00:16:28.511
get you up in the morning

00:16:28.511 --> 00:16:32.177
and help you read that research article.

00:16:32.177 --> 00:16:35.922
So run towards your energy,
but also ask yourself,

00:16:35.922 --> 00:16:39.038
"And how am I gonna get paid to do this?"

00:16:39.038 --> 00:16:41.441
And where can I uniquely plug in?

00:16:41.441 --> 00:16:45.002
Where can I make the most
difference, follow my energy,

00:16:45.002 --> 00:16:48.998
but realistically be
able to build a career

00:16:48.998 --> 00:16:52.859
and some wellbeing that's
gonna be sustainable.

00:16:52.859 --> 00:16:55.870
So I never really had a path like,

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:57.977
"Oh, I definitely wanna
work in philanthropy,

00:16:57.977 --> 00:16:59.578
"or I definitely wanna do this or that."

00:16:59.578 --> 00:17:04.301
But I've used more of those
guidelines and characteristics.

00:17:04.301 --> 00:17:05.134
- And definitely is.

00:17:05.134 --> 00:17:07.832
I mean, there's just even
the story you told us,

00:17:07.832 --> 00:17:11.536
the fact that you read about
the Gates Foundation starting,

00:17:11.536 --> 00:17:14.155
I think a lot of the world
probably read about it,

00:17:14.155 --> 00:17:15.465
but there's something and you said,

00:17:15.465 --> 00:17:17.430
"I want them to hire me."

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:18.263
But I think-

00:17:18.263 --> 00:17:19.397
- And they said, no.

00:17:19.397 --> 00:17:20.388
- It's a mindset.

00:17:20.388 --> 00:17:21.602
And they said, no? Okay.

00:17:21.602 --> 00:17:22.458
They said, no.

00:17:22.458 --> 00:17:23.559
And then what happened?

00:17:23.559 --> 00:17:25.449
- They said no so many times.

00:17:25.449 --> 00:17:28.505
So the article came out,
and the Gates Foundation

00:17:28.505 --> 00:17:31.462
was just starting and it was
very much in stealth mode.

00:17:31.462 --> 00:17:36.277
And so they said that reporter
was led into their office,

00:17:36.277 --> 00:17:39.976
which was in a above a pizza
shop in Redmond, Washington.

00:17:39.976 --> 00:17:43.291
And so I drove over to
Redmond, Washington,

00:17:43.291 --> 00:17:45.177
and I looked at all the pizza shops

00:17:45.177 --> 00:17:47.246
and I look for one with
an office above it.

00:17:47.246 --> 00:17:49.542
And I basically figured
out where they were.

00:17:49.542 --> 00:17:52.599
And then I would go there every day

00:17:52.599 --> 00:17:56.562
and I would shove my
resume and my cover letter

00:17:56.562 --> 00:17:59.518
into their mailbox and make a case for

00:17:59.518 --> 00:18:03.733
why they needed to hire
me and why I would be

00:18:03.733 --> 00:18:05.531
so great on their team.

00:18:05.531 --> 00:18:08.936
And then they would email me
back a very polite rejection.

00:18:08.936 --> 00:18:10.710
And that happened like three times.

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:12.219
And this is a true story, Sal.

00:18:12.219 --> 00:18:13.425
This is a true story.

00:18:13.425 --> 00:18:15.452
And then after the third time,

00:18:15.452 --> 00:18:19.236
literally I got this
email from Jack Barris,

00:18:19.236 --> 00:18:23.097
who said I will meet you for 15 minutes

00:18:23.097 --> 00:18:25.422
at Starbucks in Redmond,

00:18:25.422 --> 00:18:28.755
if you promise to never bother us again.

00:18:29.683 --> 00:18:30.846
- Wow.

00:18:30.846 --> 00:18:32.668
- And he ended up hiring me.

00:18:32.668 --> 00:18:35.418
You stalked the Gates Foundation.

00:18:37.191 --> 00:18:38.078
- I was persistent.

00:18:38.078 --> 00:18:38.911
I showed some grit.

00:18:38.911 --> 00:18:40.918
- You were persistent.

00:18:40.918 --> 00:18:44.366
- I made the case of why
I brought them value.

00:18:44.366 --> 00:18:47.383
I've learned that you don't make the case,

00:18:47.383 --> 00:18:49.922
young people, people who
are trying to get a job,

00:18:49.922 --> 00:18:54.277
do not make the case of why
such and such is your dream job.

00:18:54.277 --> 00:18:57.387
Don't convince Sal, that
working at the Khan Academy

00:18:57.387 --> 00:19:00.374
is your dream job convince Sal,

00:19:00.374 --> 00:19:02.546
that you are gonna be a value to him,

00:19:02.546 --> 00:19:04.933
that you are going to
make his life easier,

00:19:04.933 --> 00:19:08.799
that you are bringing a skillset
that he desperately needs.

00:19:08.799 --> 00:19:11.831
'Cause that's one thing
I've learned as an employer

00:19:11.831 --> 00:19:15.243
and sitting on the
other side of the table.

00:19:15.243 --> 00:19:16.139
- And that's right.

00:19:16.139 --> 00:19:19.393
As much as you know, I would
like to believe that yes,

00:19:19.393 --> 00:19:20.954
you are directly drawn to people

00:19:20.954 --> 00:19:23.039
who can make your life easier

00:19:23.039 --> 00:19:26.705
and who can help fulfill a
broader mission or vision

00:19:26.705 --> 00:19:29.161
that you are part of or invested in.

00:19:29.161 --> 00:19:30.151
Well, I am curious.

00:19:30.151 --> 00:19:32.566
I mean, this is a much
more interesting story

00:19:32.566 --> 00:19:34.579
than I expected when we kind of started

00:19:34.579 --> 00:19:35.438
double clicking on it.

00:19:35.438 --> 00:19:37.617
What was that meeting like?

00:19:37.617 --> 00:19:40.333
I mean, this person who's kind
of trying to get rid of you.

00:19:40.333 --> 00:19:42.352
You've been sending them a resumes,

00:19:42.352 --> 00:19:44.498
not taking no for an answer.

00:19:44.498 --> 00:19:46.951
I'm assuming he didn't
have the posture of like,

00:19:46.951 --> 00:19:49.804
"I'm really excited
about this conversation."

00:19:49.804 --> 00:19:52.053
- No, but he was a gracious man.

00:19:52.053 --> 00:19:53.886
That's why I name him.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:20:00.409
And look, I also had to be
flexible on my end, right?

00:20:00.409 --> 00:20:01.497
I had a degree.

00:20:01.497 --> 00:20:03.566
I had published research.

00:20:03.566 --> 00:20:05.033
I was funded by the Ford Foundation

00:20:05.033 --> 00:20:08.193
to do some of my own research.

00:20:08.193 --> 00:20:09.634
And when I was pitching myself,

00:20:09.634 --> 00:20:11.112
I was pitching myself more as,

00:20:11.112 --> 00:20:13.524
"Hey, I can help with econometrics.

00:20:13.524 --> 00:20:16.739
"I can do measurement and evaluation."

00:20:16.739 --> 00:20:20.142
There are all these various public policy.

00:20:20.142 --> 00:20:20.975
And he was like,

00:20:20.975 --> 00:20:24.351
"Yeah, I don't think we're
ready actually for advocacy

00:20:24.351 --> 00:20:26.843
"or public policy and I
don't actually even know

00:20:26.843 --> 00:20:29.169
what econometrics are but thank you."

00:20:29.169 --> 00:20:31.076
But he started to think, and he was like,

00:20:31.076 --> 00:20:32.752
"You're a very good writer.

00:20:32.752 --> 00:20:36.159
"And we actually need someone
to write our brochure."

00:20:36.159 --> 00:20:38.085
And so I was literally hired,

00:20:38.085 --> 00:20:39.693
in order to write their brochure.

00:20:39.693 --> 00:20:42.593
So a couple of lessons here,
especially for women and girls

00:20:42.593 --> 00:20:44.785
who might be listening.

00:20:44.785 --> 00:20:46.611
One thing is

00:20:46.611 --> 00:20:48.980
I think sometimes we have
to get our foot in the door

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:51.661
and being willing to be
a little bit flexible.

00:20:51.661 --> 00:20:53.716
And the other thing is when he said,

00:20:53.716 --> 00:20:57.512
we need someone to write our
first brochure and to design it

00:20:57.512 --> 00:21:00.346
and work with vendors
and get it published.

00:21:00.346 --> 00:21:01.179
And he said,

00:21:01.179 --> 00:21:03.205
"Is that something you can do?"

00:21:03.205 --> 00:21:06.558
And I started to do the
typical girl thing, like,

00:21:06.558 --> 00:21:09.062
"Well, I've never done that before,

00:21:09.062 --> 00:21:11.257
but I'd be happy to try."

00:21:11.257 --> 00:21:15.334
Some spirit came up out
of me and I was like,

00:21:15.334 --> 00:21:18.186
"Absolutely, I can do that."

00:21:18.186 --> 00:21:19.214
And so I was hired

00:21:19.214 --> 00:21:22.573
on contract to do their first
brochure and it went okay.

00:21:22.573 --> 00:21:24.617
And then they were like,

00:21:24.617 --> 00:21:26.223
"We actually need someone now

00:21:26.223 --> 00:21:27.982
"to create our first website.

00:21:27.982 --> 00:21:29.445
"Is that something you can do?"

00:21:29.445 --> 00:21:33.014
And again, so I had zero
experience with websites.

00:21:33.014 --> 00:21:34.270
I barely understood the internet

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:36.389
and could function on email at the time.

00:21:36.389 --> 00:21:37.222
But I said,

00:21:37.222 --> 00:21:40.243
"Absolutely, I can
absolutely build a website."

00:21:40.243 --> 00:21:43.918
And so I think sometimes just
being brave, taking that step

00:21:43.918 --> 00:21:47.999
believing in yourself and
then eventually I got hired.

00:21:47.999 --> 00:21:48.832
And when I left,

00:21:48.832 --> 00:21:51.554
I was the Deputy Director
of the Global Health Program

00:21:51.554 --> 00:21:52.388
eight years later.

00:21:52.388 --> 00:21:54.786
And I used to bring Bill and Melinda Gates

00:21:54.786 --> 00:21:58.276
to Africa and India and
Pakistan and all over the world

00:21:58.276 --> 00:21:59.364
for weeks at a time to do

00:21:59.364 --> 00:22:02.282
their learning and their
education, advocacy trips.

00:22:02.282 --> 00:22:06.152
So really appreciate
that Gates was willing to

00:22:06.152 --> 00:22:09.152
take a bet on me early in my career.

00:22:10.901 --> 00:22:13.401
- No, and there's a lot of ...

00:22:15.117 --> 00:22:17.683
Because we learn a little lesson is

00:22:17.683 --> 00:22:20.662
you had some real skills
that you had built over time.

00:22:20.662 --> 00:22:23.731
And so the real message
for those young people,

00:22:23.731 --> 00:22:25.630
I feel old now whenever I say that,

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:28.592
but for those young
young people listening is

00:22:28.592 --> 00:22:31.311
build up your skills as well as possible.

00:22:31.311 --> 00:22:33.236
And in that, I think the writing skill,

00:22:33.236 --> 00:22:35.298
the critical thinking skill and just

00:22:35.298 --> 00:22:37.381
the can-do witness skill,

00:22:40.064 --> 00:22:43.662
then with a little sprinkle of resilience

00:22:43.662 --> 00:22:47.795
and self confidence can go a
long way is what I'm hearing.

00:22:47.795 --> 00:22:48.628
- Yeah.

00:22:48.628 --> 00:22:50.706
And especially I get
asked a lot from people

00:22:50.706 --> 00:22:53.170
who really wanna have a
career in social impact.

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:55.097
And I bet you get this a lot to Sal,

00:22:55.097 --> 00:22:57.706
where people are saying they're
coming fresh out of college,

00:22:57.706 --> 00:22:59.771
maybe a few years out and they're saying,

00:22:59.771 --> 00:23:01.317
"I wanna make a difference.

00:23:01.317 --> 00:23:04.050
"I really wanna invest
my whole self, my career

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:06.171
"in social justice or making an impact,

00:23:06.171 --> 00:23:08.404
"changing the world,
changing climate change."

00:23:08.404 --> 00:23:12.351
I often tell them the most
important thing you can do

00:23:12.351 --> 00:23:17.351
right now is to build and
develop a tangible hard skill.

00:23:17.757 --> 00:23:19.332
And that hard skill can be writing,

00:23:19.332 --> 00:23:20.565
it can be critical writing.

00:23:20.565 --> 00:23:21.990
It can be coding,

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:24.262
it can be finance and auditing.

00:23:24.262 --> 00:23:25.345
It can be PR,

00:23:27.605 --> 00:23:30.438
be an excellent product manager,

00:23:30.438 --> 00:23:32.581
but really the nonprofit sector,

00:23:32.581 --> 00:23:36.870
the government, philanthropy,
the do-gooder sector

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:40.218
needs people with hard
skills and experience.

00:23:40.218 --> 00:23:43.782
So invest in yourself,
develop those skills

00:23:43.782 --> 00:23:48.782
and do that first and bring
those into your lifetime career.

00:23:51.465 --> 00:23:53.614
- And there's a question
here from YouTube,

00:23:53.614 --> 00:23:56.697
from a Jasper Not Even a Rapper says,

00:23:59.647 --> 00:24:02.337
"Jacquelline, what would you
say to help market yourself

00:24:02.337 --> 00:24:04.999
"in a climate where your
degree might not apply

00:24:04.999 --> 00:24:05.908
"or be useful skill?"

00:24:05.908 --> 00:24:07.173
And this is a very relevant
question right now,

00:24:07.173 --> 00:24:09.485
obviously we're at record
levels of unemployment.

00:24:09.485 --> 00:24:11.616
There's new jobs, new
industries all the time

00:24:11.616 --> 00:24:13.068
that have no degrees for them.

00:24:13.068 --> 00:24:15.403
How does someone transition
or if their resume

00:24:15.403 --> 00:24:16.977
doesn't say that they're meant for that.

00:24:16.977 --> 00:24:19.869
How do they convince folks?

00:24:19.869 --> 00:24:21.580
- Great question, Jasper.

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:24.437
You heard my story and the fact that

00:24:24.437 --> 00:24:28.305
I had to believe in myself
and be willing to really learn

00:24:28.305 --> 00:24:32.344
and do different things
and come into a job

00:24:32.344 --> 00:24:34.588
where I was gonna have
a steep learning curve.

00:24:34.588 --> 00:24:37.384
So I think part of it is
that flexibility on our part.

00:24:37.384 --> 00:24:38.918
As people, I think also ...

00:24:38.918 --> 00:24:42.835
Look, the world is changing
so much and degrees,

00:24:42.835 --> 00:24:45.034
especially what major you were in,

00:24:45.034 --> 00:24:47.069
what grades you got in your major.

00:24:47.069 --> 00:24:48.499
I think these kinds of things are gonna

00:24:48.499 --> 00:24:49.779
matter less and less,

00:24:49.779 --> 00:24:53.006
and it's gonna be a lot
about lifelong learning.

00:24:53.006 --> 00:24:56.716
So using resources like
Khan Academy, for example,

00:24:56.716 --> 00:25:00.698
learning, taking new
skills, new certifications

00:25:00.698 --> 00:25:03.770
are gonna make you very
relevant in the job market.

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:05.572
I will say the other thing Jasper,

00:25:05.572 --> 00:25:07.378
is that we need to change.

00:25:07.378 --> 00:25:09.922
The employers need to change.

00:25:09.922 --> 00:25:13.672
We need to get off of
our fixation on degrees

00:25:15.382 --> 00:25:19.386
and four year degrees really,
and actually hire for skills.

00:25:19.386 --> 00:25:20.896
And that's gonna really help us to have

00:25:20.896 --> 00:25:23.154
a more diverse workforce,

00:25:23.154 --> 00:25:26.228
it's gonna really help
us build better products.

00:25:26.228 --> 00:25:29.915
And so at Google, for example,
one thing that we have done

00:25:29.915 --> 00:25:33.143
is to really try to
reevaluate our requirements

00:25:33.143 --> 00:25:37.264
for our jobs and to really
state a four-year college degree

00:25:37.264 --> 00:25:40.268
not required or CS major not required,

00:25:40.268 --> 00:25:43.946
if you have the skills at
hand needed for this role.

00:25:43.946 --> 00:25:48.663
So there's movement that's
needed on both sides of that.

00:25:48.663 --> 00:25:50.022
- No, that's super valuable.

00:25:50.022 --> 00:25:51.600
And I realized these conversations,

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:54.359
they always go away faster
than I expect them to because

00:25:54.359 --> 00:25:55.707
I could talk for another hour,

00:25:55.707 --> 00:25:57.801
just even on the career aspect of it.

00:25:57.801 --> 00:25:59.208
But I wanna make sure that
there's space to talk about

00:25:59.208 --> 00:26:02.333
kind of the future of
Google.org and your work there.

00:26:02.333 --> 00:26:03.754
And maybe we can even connect to that.

00:26:03.754 --> 00:26:05.076
There's this question from YouTube,

00:26:05.076 --> 00:26:06.131
from Neupro Mitha,

00:26:06.131 --> 00:26:07.809
"How hard is it to run a company?"

00:26:07.809 --> 00:26:10.318
I mean, that goes into a
whole kind of your day to day

00:26:10.318 --> 00:26:12.586
and what are your stresses or your hopes

00:26:12.586 --> 00:26:16.251
and maybe how does that flow
into what you hope Google.org

00:26:16.251 --> 00:26:19.334
is able to do over the years to come?

00:26:20.996 --> 00:26:22.893
- Well, someone said to me once,

00:26:22.893 --> 00:26:24.658
and I didn't believe it at the time.

00:26:24.658 --> 00:26:26.333
I thought they were making it up,

00:26:26.333 --> 00:26:28.689
but it it was a female executive.

00:26:28.689 --> 00:26:30.694
I'm forgetting who, but she said,

00:26:30.694 --> 00:26:33.731
it's actually easier to
be CEO than it was to be

00:26:33.731 --> 00:26:36.016
like a line director.

00:26:36.016 --> 00:26:37.653
It's actually easier sometimes

00:26:37.653 --> 00:26:39.081
as you move up the food chain

00:26:39.081 --> 00:26:41.125
because you're able to
control your schedule more,

00:26:41.125 --> 00:26:42.377
control your life more,

00:26:42.377 --> 00:26:44.265
focus on the things that you're good at

00:26:44.265 --> 00:26:47.011
and hire excellent people
to cover all of the areas

00:26:47.011 --> 00:26:48.940
that you're really not good at.

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:50.631
And so I would say that that's true

00:26:50.631 --> 00:26:52.856
and I see that as a word of encouragement

00:26:52.856 --> 00:26:56.218
to people who are maybe wondering
if they have what it takes

00:26:56.218 --> 00:27:00.035
to go to that next level to be CEO or COO,

00:27:00.035 --> 00:27:03.483
or to run the show at whatever level.

00:27:03.483 --> 00:27:06.460
And I think sometimes when
you're looking up at that.

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:07.391
and you think,

00:27:07.391 --> 00:27:09.671
"Well, I'm working so hard right now,

00:27:09.671 --> 00:27:11.603
"and I'm so stressed right now.

00:27:11.603 --> 00:27:13.630
"It must be 10X at that level."

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:15.607
Actually, sometimes it's easier

00:27:15.607 --> 00:27:17.701
because you have more control.

00:27:17.701 --> 00:27:22.230
So that's just something to
consider about the question

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:23.842
about being the boss

00:27:23.842 --> 00:27:27.531
and I've always believed
also I was told early on

00:27:27.531 --> 00:27:30.409
hire people who are smarter
and better than you are,

00:27:30.409 --> 00:27:31.917
don't be threatened by them.

00:27:31.917 --> 00:27:36.475
And so I have always
tried to hire an A-team.

00:27:36.475 --> 00:27:41.078
My deputies are smarter
better than me in so many ways

00:27:41.078 --> 00:27:45.331
and empowering your people
so that you're not alone.

00:27:45.331 --> 00:27:48.529
There's not this loneliness at the top

00:27:48.529 --> 00:27:50.298
or feeling that you're carrying the weight

00:27:50.298 --> 00:27:51.931
of Atlas on your shoulders.

00:27:51.931 --> 00:27:55.208
I've built a very
collaborative leadership team

00:27:55.208 --> 00:27:58.215
where I feel like we are
carrying the load together.

00:27:58.215 --> 00:28:00.457
I have people like Hannah
Peter, for example,

00:28:00.457 --> 00:28:02.537
who's running our education portfolio

00:28:02.537 --> 00:28:04.206
and has done a lot of the work

00:28:04.206 --> 00:28:06.137
that we've been doing more recently

00:28:06.137 --> 00:28:08.602
with Khan Academy with DonorsChoose

00:28:08.602 --> 00:28:11.472
and being able to trust someone like her,

00:28:11.472 --> 00:28:13.224
Andrew Dunkelman and say,

00:28:13.224 --> 00:28:14.779
"Hey, you all have this,

00:28:14.779 --> 00:28:19.115
"I'm gonna just listen to
your recommendations here."

00:28:19.115 --> 00:28:23.890
Has been amazing in terms
of increasing the impact

00:28:23.890 --> 00:28:25.563
that Google.org can have,

00:28:25.563 --> 00:28:29.172
and frankly helping me
to be a better leader

00:28:29.172 --> 00:28:30.923
- And what with that lens.

00:28:30.923 --> 00:28:33.474
And I think I need some coaching from you

00:28:33.474 --> 00:28:35.680
because I definitely have things

00:28:35.680 --> 00:28:38.758
that I'm always coping with.

00:28:38.758 --> 00:28:41.125
And the loneliness is part of
it sometimes if I'm honest.

00:28:41.125 --> 00:28:41.958
- Yeah.

00:28:42.842 --> 00:28:45.730
- But what is your hope for ...

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:47.009
Actually I'll broaden it

00:28:47.009 --> 00:28:49.317
for Google.org over the years to come

00:28:49.317 --> 00:28:52.362
and even for your own direct impact

00:28:52.362 --> 00:28:55.891
either through Google.org or broadly?

00:28:55.891 --> 00:29:00.101
- I mean, Sal my dream
is actually that we find

00:29:00.101 --> 00:29:05.101
and fund and support and empower
a thousand Sal Khan, right?

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:09.151
You are actually our success story, right?

00:29:09.151 --> 00:29:11.568
Because our job is to invest,

00:29:13.543 --> 00:29:17.460
provide the seed funding,
the catalytic funding

00:29:19.218 --> 00:29:20.363
that is necessary.

00:29:20.363 --> 00:29:23.190
And I think philanthropic
capital can take risks.

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:26.201
And companies like Google tech companies

00:29:26.201 --> 00:29:28.516
can be particularly useful I think

00:29:28.516 --> 00:29:31.418
when it comes to investing in technology,

00:29:31.418 --> 00:29:35.311
a lot of funders and governments
and private individuals

00:29:35.311 --> 00:29:37.413
don't feel like they know
or understand enough about,

00:29:37.413 --> 00:29:39.773
let's say technology or
artificial intelligence,

00:29:39.773 --> 00:29:42.219
they're just not going to
invest those organizations.

00:29:42.219 --> 00:29:45.182
So that's something I think
we should always ask ourselves

00:29:45.182 --> 00:29:48.520
either as individuals, as companies,

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:50.853
"Where am I uniquely gifted?

00:29:52.005 --> 00:29:53.400
"How can I make a difference

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:56.453
"that builds on my unique strengths?"

00:29:56.453 --> 00:29:59.681
So for Google.org, we are Google.

00:29:59.681 --> 00:30:01.581
We are a technology company

00:30:01.581 --> 00:30:04.670
and technology is desperately needed

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:06.640
in the social impact sector.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:08.494
So that's where we're doubling down.

00:30:08.494 --> 00:30:11.762
So when an issue comes up
like education, or COVID,

00:30:11.762 --> 00:30:14.645
we think about what are the platforms?

00:30:14.645 --> 00:30:18.165
What are the scaled ways that
are leveraging technology

00:30:18.165 --> 00:30:21.076
where we could have really
helped shift the curve?

00:30:21.076 --> 00:30:24.289
So Sal, when you came along ...

00:30:24.289 --> 00:30:26.383
I don't know if I've
ever told you this story,

00:30:26.383 --> 00:30:28.924
but part of it was that my husband, John

00:30:28.924 --> 00:30:31.288
is an elementary school teacher,

00:30:31.288 --> 00:30:34.947
and he has been a classroom
teacher for almost 30 years.

00:30:34.947 --> 00:30:38.002
And he was an early user
of the Khan Academy.

00:30:38.002 --> 00:30:40.430
- Oh, I need to send
him a box of chocolates.

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:44.939
- Oh man. A big box and with
some dark chocolate in there

00:30:44.939 --> 00:30:46.136
because his wife likes it.

00:30:46.136 --> 00:30:47.252
- Absolutely.

00:30:47.252 --> 00:30:50.400
- But I saw firsthand what
a difference Khan Academy

00:30:50.400 --> 00:30:54.733
made for a classroom teacher
and then later in life.

00:30:55.633 --> 00:30:57.986
So my daughter Hosanna who was born.

00:30:57.986 --> 00:30:59.744
I said, when I was in grad school.

00:30:59.744 --> 00:31:03.675
So she majored in computer
science actually in college.

00:31:03.675 --> 00:31:06.606
And when she needed help
with help with her homework

00:31:06.606 --> 00:31:07.500
or her problem sets,

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:08.898
or really wanted to understand something

00:31:08.898 --> 00:31:10.239
that was happening in the classroom,

00:31:10.239 --> 00:31:13.284
she went to Khan Academy and she was ...

00:31:13.284 --> 00:31:16.755
So you were tremendously
helpful in her career.

00:31:16.755 --> 00:31:21.194
So I've been personally
able to see firsthand

00:31:21.194 --> 00:31:23.088
how helpful Khan Academy is.

00:31:23.088 --> 00:31:25.622
That's why we invested in
the beginning, but then also,

00:31:25.622 --> 00:31:29.181
I benefited from those fruits
later on with my daughter

00:31:29.181 --> 00:31:32.354
where I think particularly as
a female in computer science,

00:31:32.354 --> 00:31:34.211
it was tremendously helpful for her

00:31:34.211 --> 00:31:36.797
to have a resource to go to.

00:31:36.797 --> 00:31:37.907
- No, well, that's
always wonderful to hear.

00:31:37.907 --> 00:31:39.505
And actually you never did tell,

00:31:39.505 --> 00:31:42.843
I mean, back in 2000, and
it was actually 2009 when

00:31:42.843 --> 00:31:44.542
we started first having the conversations

00:31:44.542 --> 00:31:47.591
with Google I remember, I
was kind of overwhelmed,

00:31:47.591 --> 00:31:48.717
not knowing what to make of it.

00:31:48.717 --> 00:31:49.550
I used to walk around with

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:50.937
these little like placemats slides.

00:31:50.937 --> 00:31:54.135
And I remember there was a big
room of folks that said like,

00:31:54.135 --> 00:31:55.444
"What would you do with more resources?

00:31:55.444 --> 00:31:56.402
"A lot of us have been using it.

00:31:56.402 --> 00:31:58.740
"We didn't realize you were
just some guy in a closet

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.197
"operating three miles down the road."

00:32:01.197 --> 00:32:02.030
(laughing)

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:04.223
It was all interesting time.

00:32:04.223 --> 00:32:06.468
It's fun to talk about
it in hindsight now,

00:32:06.468 --> 00:32:08.237
but yeah, it was interesting.

00:32:08.237 --> 00:32:09.256
Well, Jacquelline,

00:32:09.256 --> 00:32:12.469
I mean, we've gotta do this
again if you're up for it,

00:32:12.469 --> 00:32:15.318
but we're unfortunately all out of time.

00:32:15.318 --> 00:32:20.318
But any final thoughts,
words, words of wisdom, ideas,

00:32:20.591 --> 00:32:22.621
things you wanna throw out there.

00:32:22.621 --> 00:32:27.621
- I mean, we are in a moment
right now, not only in the US,

00:32:28.366 --> 00:32:30.518
but globally, we are in a moment.

00:32:30.518 --> 00:32:33.073
There's something like 2 billion kids

00:32:33.073 --> 00:32:34.984
who are not able to go

00:32:34.984 --> 00:32:37.396
to the traditional
schooling that they had.

00:32:37.396 --> 00:32:39.329
If there was ever a moment,

00:32:39.329 --> 00:32:44.329
for the Khan Academy to come
up and shine and be utilized.

00:32:44.475 --> 00:32:46.206
It is this moment.

00:32:46.206 --> 00:32:48.374
So I don't think I mentioned earlier,

00:32:48.374 --> 00:32:52.908
but we actually just regranted
again to Khan Academy.

00:32:52.908 --> 00:32:55.204
He did not ask me to say
this, but let me say,

00:32:55.204 --> 00:32:59.217
we just gave additional funding
in the last couple months.

00:32:59.217 --> 00:33:02.794
because we recognize
this is a global moment

00:33:02.794 --> 00:33:05.198
where we need to be investing in platforms

00:33:05.198 --> 00:33:08.696
that hit scale that are
available in local languages,

00:33:08.696 --> 00:33:10.821
in content that teachers can use.

00:33:10.821 --> 00:33:12.491
Teachers are the heroes,

00:33:12.491 --> 00:33:15.273
they are the frontline essential workers.

00:33:15.273 --> 00:33:17.424
This is a way that we
can all invest in them.

00:33:17.424 --> 00:33:18.785
So thank you, Khan Academy.

00:33:18.785 --> 00:33:19.679
Thank you, Sal.

00:33:19.679 --> 00:33:21.613
Thank you, everyone out there

00:33:21.613 --> 00:33:24.618
who is working to make
the world a better place

00:33:24.618 --> 00:33:27.134
in your own unique way.

00:33:27.134 --> 00:33:28.801
This is that moment.

00:33:29.924 --> 00:33:30.757
- Yeah.

00:33:30.757 --> 00:33:32.511
Well, obviously I couldn't agree more

00:33:32.511 --> 00:33:35.789
and Jacquelline, thank you so
much for obviously the work

00:33:35.789 --> 00:33:37.597
that you've been able to do with us

00:33:37.597 --> 00:33:40.203
and help us, help do our mission,

00:33:40.203 --> 00:33:42.135
but also well beyond that.

00:33:42.135 --> 00:33:43.229
The incredible work that you all

00:33:43.229 --> 00:33:44.205
are doing at google.org.com.

00:33:44.205 --> 00:33:45.617
Thank you so much for joining.

00:33:45.617 --> 00:33:46.450
- Thanks so much, Sal.

00:33:46.450 --> 00:33:47.350
Appreciate it.

00:33:47.350 --> 00:33:48.350
- Thank you.

00:33:49.301 --> 00:33:52.975
So thanks everyone for joining as always.

00:33:52.975 --> 00:33:54.669
My perception of time changes

00:33:54.669 --> 00:33:56.117
when we're having a good conversation.

00:33:56.117 --> 00:33:57.195
It goes by very, very fast,

00:33:57.195 --> 00:34:01.040
but as you can tell another
really, really fun conversation.

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:03.361
We're not going to be doing
a live stream tomorrow,

00:34:03.361 --> 00:34:05.609
but we will be having, I think Tuesday.

00:34:05.609 --> 00:34:07.561
We're gonna be coming back.

00:34:07.561 --> 00:34:09.690
We have two guests next week.

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:10.985
Both very, very interesting.

00:34:10.985 --> 00:34:13.768
Casey Wasserman, famous
I guess you could say,

00:34:13.768 --> 00:34:17.050
agent in the sports and
entertainment world.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:17.883
So I think it should be

00:34:17.883 --> 00:34:19.631
some really interesting
